WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.020
Know every cost: why busy is not the same as profitable.

2
00:00:04.370 --> 00:00:08.960
This is the video for Module 1 of Pricing, Costing and Profit Planning.

3
00:00:10.210 --> 00:00:17.990
Many women sell every day and still have nothing to show at the end of the month. Usually the problem is the price, not hard work.

4
00:00:18.340 --> 00:00:25.870
Revenue is all the money customers pay you. 40 bottles at 300 naira is 12,000 naira of revenue.

5
00:00:27.120 --> 00:00:33.350
Cost is all the money the business spends to make and sell, including monthly things like rent and data.

6
00:00:34.600 --> 00:00:40.060
Profit is revenue minus total cost. If the answer is negative, it is a loss.

7
00:00:41.310 --> 00:00:47.650
And remember, money in your purse is not automatically profit. Some of it is for the next bag of sugar.

8
00:00:48.900 --> 00:00:56.710
One batch makes 40 bottles. Amaka adds up the zobo leaves, spices, sugar, bottles, water, ice and gas.

9
00:00:57.960 --> 00:01:05.550
They come to 12,000 naira. Divided by 40, that is 300 naira a bottle, exactly her selling price.

10
00:01:06.800 --> 00:01:11.450
She also pays 1,500 naira to carry the cooler to the park.

11
00:01:12.700 --> 00:01:24.710
So her batch costs 13,500 naira, but sells for only 12,000 naira. She loses about 1,500 naira every batch, before counting her own time.

12
00:01:25.060 --> 00:01:27.410
Selling more would only make the loss bigger.

13
00:01:28.660 --> 00:01:35.640
These are the costs most often left out. First, your own labour. If you do not pay yourself, your price is too low.

14
00:01:36.890 --> 00:01:43.630
Packaging and labels, transport to market and to customers, and energy like gas and generator fuel.

15
00:01:44.880 --> 00:01:47.690
Phone data and airtime for WhatsApp orders.

16
00:01:48.940 --> 00:01:52.930
Wastage: burnt batches, broken items and unsold food.

17
00:01:54.180 --> 00:01:58.410
And equipment wear, market dues, and POS and transfer charges.

18
00:01:59.660 --> 00:02:11.160
Sort each cost in two ways. Variable costs rise and fall with how much you make. Make twice as much chin chin, and you use about twice as much flour, oil and packaging.

19
00:02:11.510 --> 00:02:15.730
Direct costs can be traced to one item, like the lining in one gown.

20
00:02:16.980 --> 00:02:26.260
Fixed costs stay about the same each month, like rent, a market levy and your own wage. Indirect costs, or overheads, support the whole business.

21
00:02:26.610 --> 00:02:31.500
Quick test: if I made nothing this month, would I still pay it? If yes, it is fixed.

22
00:02:32.750 --> 00:02:40.320
Halima's monthly overheads are rent, an apprentice, generator fuel and data. Together, 80,000 naira.

23
00:02:41.570 --> 00:02:47.970
The lining, zip, thread, interfacing and buttons for one gown cost 4,400 naira.

24
00:02:49.220 --> 00:02:53.740
Shared across 40 garments, each one carries 2,000 naira of overheads.

25
00:02:54.990 --> 00:03:04.190
So each gown costs 6,400 naira before she pays herself. If she charged 6,000 naira, she would lose money on every gown.

26
00:03:04.540 --> 00:03:12.470
In a slow month of 20 garments, each would carry 4,000 naira of overheads. Fixed costs do not shrink when sales fall.

27
00:03:13.720 --> 00:03:23.880
Every calculation is only as good as your numbers. Capture every receipt for two weeks, with the quantity as well as the price. Weigh and count real batches.

28
00:03:24.230 --> 00:03:30.970
Measure your wastage, keep business money in a separate account, and decide a fair monthly wage for yourself.

29
00:03:32.220 --> 00:03:42.950
Protect your money. Fraudsters send fake screenshots or SMS alerts. Confirm every payment inside your bank app or on your POS before you release goods.

30
00:03:44.200 --> 00:03:50.470
Never share your PIN, password or one-time code, even with someone who says they are bank staff.

31
00:03:51.720 --> 00:03:58.570
If someone overpays and asks for a refund, wait until the full amount shows in your app and is not reversed.

32
00:03:58.920 --> 00:04:04.530
And if a supplier sends new account details, call them on the number you already have before paying.

33
00:04:05.780 --> 00:04:22.120
To remember. Profit is revenue minus total cost. Selling more at a loss-making price only makes the loss bigger. Count your labour, packaging, data and wastage. Fixed costs stay, even in slow months. And confirm every payment in your app.

34
00:04:23.370 --> 00:04:36.700
Now find your real profit or loss on one batch or one week. Sort every cost into fixed or variable, direct or indirect. And set up your receipts envelope and write down your fair monthly wage.

35
00:04:37.050 --> 00:04:39.000
Continue with the lessons below.
